“How do you handle functions and objects in TypeScript?”
“I define strict types for function parameters and return values to ensure type safety. 
For objects, I use types or interfaces to define structure, including optional and readonly properties.
I also combine them to type APIs, props, and reusable logic.”


🔥 1. Functions in TypeScript
✅ Basic Function Typing
function add(a: number, b: number): number {
  return a + b;
}

👉 What’s happening:

a: number, b: number → parameter types
: number → return type
✅ Optional Parameters
function greet(name: string, age?: number) {
  return age ? `${name} is ${age}` : name;
}

👉 ? means optional

✅ Default Parameters
function greet(name: string = "Guest") {
  return name;
}
✅ Function Type (Very Important)
type AddFn = (a: number, b: number) => number;

const add: AddFn = (x, y) => x + y;

👉 Used a lot in:

callbacks
props in React
✅ Void & Never
function logMessage(): void {
  console.log("Hello");
}
function throwError(): never {
  throw new Error("Error");
}

👉 never = function never completes

🔥 2. Objects in TypeScript
✅ Basic Object Type
let user: { name: string; age: number } = {
  name: "John",
  age: 30
};

👉 Not scalable → use type or interface

✅ Using Type
type User = {
  name: string;
  age: number;
};

const user: User = {
  name: "John",
  age: 30
};
✅ Optional Properties
type User = {
  name: string;
  age?: number;
};
✅ Readonly Properties
type User = {
  readonly id: number;
  name: string;
};

👉 id cannot be changed

🔥 3. Nested Objects (Very Common)
type User = {
  name: string;
  address: {
    city: string;
    pincode: number;
  };
};
🔥 4. Functions Inside Objects
type User = {
  name: string;
  greet: () => string;
};

const user: User = {
  name: "John",
  greet: () => "Hello"
};
🔥 5. Dynamic Object Keys (Important)
type Scores = {
  [key: string]: number;
};

const scores: Scores = {
  math: 90,
  science: 85
};

👉 Used in:

dictionaries
API maps
🔥 6. Objects + Functions Together (REAL INTERVIEW)
type User = {
  name: string;
  age: number;
};

function printUser(user: User): string {
  return `${user.name} is ${user.age}`;
}
🔥 7. Destructuring with Types
function greet({ name, age }: { name: string; age: number }) {
  return `${name} is ${age}`;
}
🔥 8. Using in React (CRITICAL)
type Props = {
  name: string;
  onClick: () => void;
};

function Button({ name, onClick }: Props) {
  return <button onClick={onClick}>{name}</button>;
}
🧠 Key Interview Concepts
1. Why type functions?

👉 Prevent wrong inputs

2. Why type objects?

👉 Ensure consistent data structure

3. Common mistakes:
Using any instead of proper types
Not typing function return
Overcomplicating simple types


